850
01:20:35.030 --> 01:20:44.359
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Once upon a time, there was an old woman dressed all in black, who was the godmother to the sun, and to every sun that crossed the sky every day.

851
01:20:45.340 --> 01:20:47.180
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Whether waking or dreaming.

852
01:20:47.480 --> 01:20:53.719
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): No one who saw her face could remember or explain what it was they had seen, so they called it Shadow.

853
01:20:54.740 --> 01:20:56.490
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): She did not live in a house.

854
01:20:56.620 --> 01:20:58.749
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): But sat beside the one gate

855
01:20:59.020 --> 01:21:05.149
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Which sits in the fence that divides night from day, the twilight gate that keeps the two apart from each other.

856
01:21:06.390 --> 01:21:22.159
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Each time a bright sun was born, it passed through the world, and over the village, and through the gate into the darkness to the place where all stars go after they've gone out, and each time the old woman would open the gate and gently greet her son by its name as it went through.

857
01:21:22.850 --> 01:21:27.740
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Until one day, a boy in the village fell in love with the sons that went by.

858
01:21:28.330 --> 01:21:32.539
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): All day long he watched them, and cried when they passed beyond his sight.

859
01:21:33.040 --> 01:21:44.849
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): So he fashioned a net at the end of a tall pole, and caught the sun as it passed, and put it in a box just for himself, not knowing that the old woman was waiting for her child at the gate that divides night from day.

860
01:21:46.170 --> 01:21:51.440
